data: {"id":"gen-1793287278-Ug4UqMeCPMy9pfsPmKdc","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1793287278,"model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017","provider":"Ionstream","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"","role":"assistant","annotations":[{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://collection.sl.nsw.gov.au/record/74VKpg4MArWy","title":"Le Roman de Silence : a thirteenth-century Arthurian verse-romance / edited by Lewis Thorpe. | Collection - State Library of NSW","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"Le Roman de Silence : a thirteenth-century Arthurian verse-romance / edited by Lewis Thorpe.\n\nHow to access this item\n\nCall number: T0352837\nLocation: Request from offsite storage - Collect after 4pm next weekday (onsite use only)\nCall number: T0353338\n\n...\n\nLe Roman de Silence : a thirteenth-century Arthurian verse-romance / edited by Lewis Thorpe.\n\nhttps://collection.sl.nsw.gov.au/record/74VKpg4MArWy\n\nLe Roman de Silence : a thirteenth-century Arthurian verse-romance / edited by Lewis Thorpe.\n\nAbout this item\n\nFull title\nLe Roman de Silence : a thirteenth-century Arthurian verse-romance / edited by Lewis Thorpe.\nAuthor / Creator\nHeldris, de Cornuälle\nPublisher\nCambridge : Heffer, 1972.\nDate\n1972.\nCall Numbers\nT0352837 , T0353338\nRecord Identifier\n74VKpg4MArWy\nMMS ID\n9910 1935916970 2626\nLanguage\n* Multiple languages\n* French, Old (ca. 842-1300)\n* English\nFormats\nBooks\n\nPhysical Description\n\nPhysical content\nvi, 256 p. : facsim. ; 23 cm.\n\nPublication information\n\nPublisher\nCambridge : Heffer, 1972.\nPlace of Publication\nEngland\nDate Published\n1972.\n\nSubjects\n\nSubjects and topics\n* Merlin (Legendary character)--Romances\n\n...\n\nAuthors, Artists and Contributors\n\nAuthor / Creator\nHeldris, de Cornuälle\nAuthor / Artists\nThorpe, Lewis G. M., 1913- ed.\n\nNotes\n\nGeneral note\n\"The only extant copy of the poem forms part of a thirteenth-century manuscript ... in the Muniments Room of the University of Nottingham ... number Mi.LM.6.\"\nOld French text; English introd."}}]},"finish_reason":null,"native_finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"id":"gen-1793287278-Ug4UqMeCPMy9pfsPmKdc","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1793287278,"model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017","provider":"Ionstream","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"","role":"assistant","annotations":[{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://www.medievalists.net/2026/07/10-lost-medieval-texts-rediscovered-centuries-later","title":"10 Lost Medieval Texts Rediscovered Centuries Later - Medievalists.net","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"They are now divided among institutions around the world. Their discovery transformed knowledge of medieval China, Buddhism and everyday life along the Silk Roads.\n\n3. Le Roman de Silence\n\nDepiction of Heldris de Cornuälle in the manuscript of Le Roman de Silence\nLe Roman de Silence is a thirteenth-century French verse romance attributed to an otherwise unknown writer named Heldris de Cornuälle. Its central character, Silence, is born female but raised as a boy after the king of England prohibits women from inheriting property. Silence becomes an accomplished musician, warrior and knight while the allegorical figures Nature and Nurture debate which identity should prevail.\n\n...\n\nBy the nineteenth century, much of the Wollaton archive had been packed into hundreds of boxes. Many were dismissed with labels such as “illegible” or “of no value.” In 1911, W. H. Stevenson visited the house on behalf of the Historical Manuscripts Commission and began examining its neglected records. Among them he identified the manuscript containing the only surviving copy of Le Roman de Silence.\nThe text remained little studied for decades, with its first complete modern edition not appearing until 1972. It has since attracted extensive attention for its treatment of inheritance, education and medieval ideas about gender. A romance that had nearly disappeared among supposedly worthless papers is now considered one of the most unusual works of medieval French literature."}}]},"finish_reason":null,"native_finish_reason":null}]}

data: {"id":"gen-1793287278-Ug4UqMeCPMy9pfsPmKdc","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1793287278,"model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash-1017","provider":"Ionstream","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"content":"","role":"assistant","annotations":[{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://repository.lib.fsu.edu/islandora/object/fsu:911249","title":"Par ma Destre | FSU Digital Repository","start_index":0,"end_index":0,"content":"Permalink: https://purl.lib.fsu.edu/diginole/honors_thesis_submission-0c9a3529-553f-4434-adbc-73e554f7c128\nStyle APA Chicago MLA Turabian\nChoose the citation style.\nDavis, S. D. (2024). Par ma Destre: Oath-Making, Breaking, Subversion, and Transgression in Le Roman de Silence. Retrieved from https://purl.lib.fsu.edu/diginole/honors_thesis_submission-0c9a3529-553f-4434-adbc-73e554f7c128 PREVIEW Datastream\nDownload...\n* Description\nFull Title\nPar ma Destre: Oath-Making, Breaking, Subversion, and Transgression in Le Roman de Silence\nNames\nDavis, Sophie Diane (author)\nDate Issued\n2024-04-15\nFormat\ntext bachelor thesis\nAbstract\nLe Roman de Silence, a nearly 7,000 line long French medieval romance, has survived since the thirteenth century in a single copy. There is not a substantial amount of evidence as to the history of the physical text, yet through analysis and comparison of the manuscript and historical records, we do know some key details. In 1911, Silence was found at Wollaton Hall in Nottingham, England (Stevenson, Historical Manuscript Commission). Now, over one-hundred years later, it is held in folios 188 recto to 223 recto of MS. Mi.LM.6 at the University of Nottingham.\n\n...\n\nIX de Laval, ca. 1286. After the wedding it is believed to have remained in the château de Laval. Scholars can assume this manuscript moved to England as a result of plundering associated with the"}}]},"finish_reason":null,"native_finish_reason":null}]}

